<doc><paragraph heading='1'><text>J20. &quot;A vegan world is utopian&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Thesis:</bold></text><text> the argument confuses the feasibility of a total, immediate change with present individual duty. Perhaps the world will never be entirely vegan, just as crime has never disappeared. But that doesn&#x27;t change what each person should do today. Many moral changes seemed utopian before they happened, and the relevant question isn&#x27;t whether everyone will change, but whether I should.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Family:</bold></text><text> J. Practicality, economy and society · </text><text><bold>Part:</bold></text><text> IND</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>1. Formulation</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Everyday version:</bold></text><text> &quot;That will never happen. It&#x27;s a hippie dream.&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Strongest version (steelman):</bold></text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>P1.</bold></text><text> A fully vegan world would require enormous changes in cultures, economies and habits.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>P2.</bold></text><text> Those changes are unlikely in the foreseeable future.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>P3.</bold></text><text> It makes no sense to guide conduct by an unattainable ideal.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>C.</bold></text><text> So veganism is a utopia and shouldn&#x27;t guide behavior.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph><text>P1 and P2 may be true. The problem lies in P3: individual duty doesn&#x27;t depend on everyone fulfilling it.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The argument is </text><text><bold>valid</bold></text><text>. Its </text><text><bold>soundness</bold></text><text> fails at P3.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>2. How it can show up</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Context</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Typical form</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;That will never happen.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;It&#x27;s a hippie dream.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Everyday conversation</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;The world isn&#x27;t going to go vegan.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Social media</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;Veganism is a utopia for people who don&#x27;t know the real world.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Academic version</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;Unattainable ideals don&#x27;t create obligations.&quot;</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Disguised version</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>&quot;One person doesn&#x27;t make a difference.&quot; (which is K1)</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>3. Answer for children</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>Maybe not everyone in the world will stop eating meat. But that doesn&#x27;t change what each of us can do. Not everyone is kind, and it&#x27;s still worth being kind. Every animal who stops suffering matters.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>4. Short answer</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>Perhaps the world will never be entirely vegan, just as crime has never disappeared. But each person&#x27;s duty doesn&#x27;t depend on everyone fulfilling it. Abolishing slavery, giving women the vote and protecting children from labor all once seemed utopian. The relevant question isn&#x27;t whether everyone will change, but whether I should. And each person who changes spares real animals, today.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Question to ask back:</bold></text><text> &quot;If the world will never be perfect, does that excuse you from doing what&#x27;s right?&quot;</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>5. Full answer</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(a) Reconstruction and diagnosis</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The argument confuses two questions:</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Question</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Type</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Answer</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Will the whole world go vegan?</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Prediction</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Uncertain</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Should I stop eating animals?</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Moral</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Doesn&#x27;t depend on the prediction</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='3'><text>(b) Where the argument breaks</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>1. Duty doesn&#x27;t depend on everyone joining in.</bold></text><text> We don&#x27;t stop condemning violence because it will never end.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>2. Many changes seemed utopian.</bold></text><text> The abolition of slavery, women&#x27;s suffrage and the protection of children were treated as dreams before they happened.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>3. Individual impact is real.</bold></text><text> Each person who changes reduces demand and spares animals (K1).</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>4. The world can change partially.</bold></text><text> Even without an entirely vegan world, the reduction in animal consumption could be enormous.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>5. The argument changes the question.</bold></text><text> It answers a prediction, not the moral question.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(c) Data and sources</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Source</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>What it says</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Note</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://campuspress.yale.edu/shellykagan/papers">Kagan, *Do I Make a Difference?* (2011)</link></text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Each purchase has a real expected effect on production</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Shows the value of individual action</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://ourworldindata.org/land-use-diets">Our World in Data, land use and diets</link></text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>A global vegan diet would reduce farmland by about 75%</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Shows the change is feasible in principle</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='3'><text>(d) The strongest counter-reply: unrealistic ideals breed frustration</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The most serious version says that championing an unattainable ideal can frustrate people and put them off, and that more modest goals would be more effective.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='3'><text>(e) Why the counter-reply doesn&#x27;t reach the conclusion</text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>It&#x27;s a strategic question, not a moral one.</bold></text><text> The best way to communicate can be debated without denying the duty.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Individual duty is attainable.</bold></text><text> Each person can stop eating animals, even if the world doesn&#x27;t change.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Ideals guide change.</bold></text><text> Even when they&#x27;re never fully realized.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph heading='3'><text>(f) Back to the moral question</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The question about the world&#x27;s future is interesting, but it doesn&#x27;t replace the question about the present: today, for those who can choose, what justifies paying for animals to be killed?</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>6. Connections</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Direction</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Code</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Why it appears</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>H10 &quot;Agriculture can&#x27;t work without manure&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Dependence becomes impossibility</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>J19 &quot;What would we do with the animals if everyone went vegan?&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>A vegan world is utopian</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes before</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>L23 &quot;Most vegans give up&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>A vegan world is utopian</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes after</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>K1 &quot;One person doesn&#x27;t make a difference&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>One person makes no difference</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Comes after</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>K9 &quot;I&#x27;ll wait for cultivated meat&quot;</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>I&#x27;ll wait for technology</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>7. Reasoning errors</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Level</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Error</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>How it appears in J20</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Logical</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Changing the question</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Answers a prediction, not a moral question</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Logical</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Nirvana fallacy</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>If not everyone can reach the ideal, nothing need be done</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Moral</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Duty conditional on universal adherence</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>It would only make sense to act if everyone acted</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>8. Contradictions</text></paragraph><list kind='ordered'><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Does the right thing on other matters, even if others don&#x27;t.</bold></text><text> Such as not stealing.</text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Admires historical changes that seemed utopian.</bold></text></paragraph></listItem><listItem><paragraph><text><bold>Knows they can change their own plate.</bold></text><text> Regardless of the world.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph heading='2'><text>9. Biases</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Bias</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>How it sustains J20</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Evidence</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Status quo bias</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>The current world seems unchangeable</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text><link href="https://ideas.repec.org/a/kap/jrisku/v1y1988i1p7-59.html">Samuelson and Zeckhauser, 1988</link></text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Diffusion of responsibility</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>If not everyone changes, no one needs to</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>General literature on diffusion of responsibility</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>10. Extrapolations</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>The principle being tested is: &quot;if a goal won&#x27;t be reached by everyone, no one has a duty to pursue it&quot;.</text></paragraph><table><row><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Applied to</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>What would follow</text></paragraph></cell><cell header='true'><paragraph><text>Note</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Ending violence</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>No one would need to stop being violent</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>No one accepts this</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Honesty</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>No one would need to be honest</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>No one accepts this</text></paragraph></cell></row><row><cell><paragraph><text>Abolishing slavery</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>It shouldn&#x27;t have been pursued</text></paragraph></cell><cell><paragraph><text>Absurd</text></paragraph></cell></row></table><paragraph heading='2'><text>11. What the argument gets right</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>It&#x27;s true that an entirely vegan world seems distant, and that cultural changes of this size are slow and uncertain. Being realistic about the pace of change helps plan better strategies.</text></paragraph><paragraph><text>But the distance from the ideal doesn&#x27;t change what each person can do today. Individual duty doesn&#x27;t wait for the world to change.</text></paragraph><paragraph heading='2'><text>References</text></paragraph><paragraph><text><bold>Philosophy</bold></text></paragraph><list kind='bullet'><listItem><paragraph><text><link href="https://campuspress.yale.edu/shellykagan/papers">Kagan, S. (2011). Do I make a difference? *Philosophy &amp; Public Affairs*, 39(2)</link></text><text>.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph><text><bold>Environmental data</bold></text></paragraph><list kind='bullet'><listItem><paragraph><text><link href="https://ourworldindata.org/land-use-diets">Ritchie, H. If the world adopted a plant-based diet, we would reduce global agricultural land use. *Our World in Data*</link></text><text>.</text></paragraph></listItem></list><paragraph><text><bold>Psychology</bold></text></paragraph><list kind='bullet'><listItem><paragraph><text><link href="https://ideas.repec.org/a/kap/jrisku/v1y1988i1p7-59.html">Samuelson, W., &amp; Zeckhauser, R. (1988). Status quo bias in decision making. *Journal of Risk and Uncertainty*, 1(1)</link></text><text>.</text></paragraph></listItem></list></doc>